cartridge "PIEMAN/B.BLACKMER/1987"

' PIEMAN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Pieman" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy! April 1987
' (Commodore 64 machine language, PIEMAN.ML at $C000, started by a
' two-line BASIC loader with SYS 49152). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/pieman-ml.dis, and the magazine's instructions), the
' game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision: a pie shop under a marquee of
' chasing bulbs, a tiled kitchen wall, a conveyor belt that really moves,
' a glowing pie oven, a wooden counter, a counterman in a white cap and
' apron and customers in hats as smooth two-colour sprites, sound effects.
'
' The game: you are the new counterman at the pie emporium. Pies come out
' of the oven on a conveyor belt. Walk up to the belt and hold the button
' as a pie passes under your hands to take it, carry it down to the counter
' and hold the button in front of the customer to sell it. A pie nobody
' takes falls off the end of the belt and is smashed. Every 25 pies sold
' the belt runs faster. When 30 pies are smashed the shop closes for the
' cleanup.
'
' From the original (orig/): two pies on the belt; the second starts when
' the first has gone part of the way, both start again when the second is
' done; a pie moves one pixel a belt step and, past the end of the belt,
' one pixel down and one along a step until it lands; the belt makes one
' step per pass at first and one more per pass every 25 pies sold (a
' pass is about two frames on the C64); the counterman moves 2 pixels a
' pass in four directions, cannot walk back up while carrying a pie, takes
' a pie with the button held when he is at the top and touches it, sells
' it with the button held at the bottom while he and the pie touch a
' customer who is standing at the counter; the customer walks away with
' the pie and a new one comes up at a random place; 30 smashed pies end
' the game; the counts of pies sold and smashed.
'
' Liberties: one pass a frame with the belt's steps counted in half steps
' (the same speeds, smooth); the belt is 160 pixels long (the C64's 194)
' and a pie falls for 28 steps, as on the C64, onto a heap that grows; the
' second pie starts halfway (94 steps; the C64's 110 of 222); touching is
' a distance test (10 pixels) instead of the C64's sprite collision
' register; a pie cannot be taken while one is carried (the C64 swapped
' them, losing the first without a count); the belt's speed stops at 8;
' the customer comes up 1 pixel a frame and leaves 2 (the C64: 0.5 and 1);
' pies are lemon or berry at random (the C64's board: TODAY'S SPECIAL
' LEMON BERRY); sounds for taking, selling, a faster belt, a customer and
' the start (the C64 had the splat and a sale tone); a best count; GAME
' OVER then the title (the C64 showed CLOSED and waited for the button).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks, either button held takes
' or sells a pie. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

